eger n, return a sorted list that has the odd numbers in collatz sequence.\n\n    The Collatz conjecture is a conjecture in mathematics that concerns a sequence defined\n    as follows: start with any positive integer n. Then each term is obtained from the \n    previous term as follows: if the previous term is even, the next term is one half of \n    the previous term. If the previous term is odd, the next term is 3 times the previous\n    term plus 1. The conjecture is that no matter what value of n, the sequence will always reach 1.\n\n    Note: \n        1. Collatz(1) is [1].\n        2. returned list sorted in increasing order.\n\n    For example:\n    get_odd_collatz(5) returns [1, 5] # The collatz sequence for 5 is [5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1], so the odd numbers are only 1, and 5.\n    \"\"\"\n    odd_numbers = set()\n    current = n\n\n    while True:\n        if current % 2 == 1:\n            odd_numbers.add(current)\n        \n        if current == 1:\n            break\n        \n        if current % 2 == 0:\n            current //= 2\n        else:\n            current = 3 * current + 1\n            \n    return sorted(list(odd_numbers))", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}, {"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "def get_odd_collatz(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a sorted list that has the odd numbers in collatz sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    odd_numbers = set()\n    current_n = n\n\n    while current_n != 1:\n        if current_n % 2 == 1:\n            odd_numbers.add(current_n)\n            current_n = 3 * current_n + 1\n        else:\n            current_n = current_n // 2\n    \n    # Always add 1, as it's the terminal odd number in the sequence\n    odd_numbers.add(1)\n\n    result_list = sorted(list(odd_numbers))\n    return result_list", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/15": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/15", "